    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Regarding the natural stone and the LVT, what specific types of damage should we be most worried about in a professional setting? Like, if there are heavy deliveries or people in hard-soled shoes walking through daily, what kind of surface degradation are we looking at over 5-10 years?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a professional setting, both natural stone and LVT can experience specific types of damage due to heavy deliveries and people in hard-soled shoes. For natural stone, the most common issues are scratches, chips, and stains. Over 5-10 years, you might see more pronounced scratches, especially in high-traffic areas. Chips can occur if heavy objects are dropped or if someone accidentally bumps into the surface. Stains from coffee, wine, or other substances can be difficult to remove and may require professional cleaning or even replacement of the affected area.\n\nLVT, on the other hand, can also suffer from scratches, but they are generally less severe than those on natural stone. Over time, the surface may become dull and lose its luster, requiring regular buffing to restore its shine. LVT is also prone to fading, especially if exposed to direct sunlight or harsh cleaning chemicals. In a high-traffic commercial lobby, both materials may also experience wear and tear from heavy furniture or equipment being moved across the floor. Regular maintenance, such as cleaning and buffing, can help mitigate these issues and extend the life of the flooring.",
      "source": "target",
